# ${SRC_DIR}/

Application code for ${PROJECT_NAME} (frontend). Stamped on ${TIMESTAMP}.

## Layout

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| [`main.tsx`](main.tsx) | React entrypoint — createRoot, providers, router. |
| [`App.tsx`](App.tsx) | Composition only. No business logic. |
| [`config/`](config/) | Env loader, query client, theme tokens. |
| [`components/`](components/) | Cross-feature UI primitives + layout shells. |
| [`features/`](features/) | Per-feature folders — see the feature-folder rule below. |
| [`hooks/`](hooks/) | Shared cross-feature hooks. |
| [`lib/`](lib/) | Framework-agnostic helpers. No React imports. |
| [`routes/`](routes/) | Router config. Each feature exports its own page component; mounting happens here. |
| [`styles/`](styles/) | Global CSS. Per-component styles co-locate with the component. |
| [`types/`](types/) | Cross-feature type declarations. |
| [`tests/`](tests/) | Unit + e2e tests + Vitest setup. |

## The strict 4-subfolder feature rule

Every feature under `features/<name>/` has **exactly these four subfolders**:

| Subfolder | What lives here | Naming suffix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `components/` | React components for the feature | `*.tsx` |
| `hooks/` | React Query hooks + plain hooks for the feature | `use*.ts` |
| `services/` | Network calls + framework-agnostic logic | `*.service.ts` |
| `types/` | Types and interfaces local to the feature | `*.types.ts` |

No file lives at the feature root. The feature's own `README.md` is the only top-level file inside a feature folder.

## Naming convention

Content must be inferable from the name:

- `HealthStatus.tsx` — React component (PascalCase).
- `useHealth.ts` — React hook (starts with `use`).
- `health.service.ts` — network / business logic.
- `health.types.ts` — request/response and domain types.
- `HealthStatus.test.tsx` — Vitest test (co-located under `tests/`).

## File size

Soft cap: **300–500 lines**. Run `npm run lint:size` to surface offenders. The check is warning-only — Compose-style screen components and generated clients can legitimately exceed the cap.
